Once the container is up and running these are the steps to import device types.

Phase 1: Data Source Setup (The Library)
This step brings the "blueprints" from GitHub into your local Postgres database.

Navigate to Core > Data Sources (or use the search bar for "Data Sources").

Add a New Source with these details:

Name: NetBox Device Type Library

Type: git

Source URL: https://github.com/netbox-community/devicetype-library.git

Ignore Rules: (Crucial for your 1Gb link) Add elevation-images/* and module-types/* to skip unnecessary bulk.

Sync: Click the Sync button and wait for the status to turn green (Completed). This populates the DataFile table in your database.

Phase 2: Script Installation (The Processor)
This step adds the logic that reads those files and creates your inventory.

Locate your NetBox Docker scripts directory (e.g., ~/homenet/netbox-docker/scripts).

Create a file named import_device_types.py and paste the Python code provided earlier.

Refresh the NetBox UI and navigate to Customization > Scripts. You should see the script listed.

Phase 3: Execution (The Import)
This is where the actual Manufacturers and Device Types are created.

Open the Script in the NetBox UI.

Configure:

Vendor Filter: Enter specific vendors (e.g., apc, cisco, dell) to keep the initial run manageable.

Commit Changes: Keep this unchecked for a "Dry Run" first.

Run Script: Click the button and check the log output.

Finalize: If the logs look good, check Commit Changes and run it again to save the devices to your database.

Phase 4: Maintenance (The Lifecycle)
Because this is a "local-first" setup, keeping it updated is simple:

Update Library: Click Sync on the Data Source. NetBox will only pull the new/changed YAML files.

Re-run Script: Run the Custom Script again. It will skip everything that already exists and only add the new models.



